+\makeatother
+
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for highlighting these distinctions. We retained the ERA5 values at 00, 06, 12 and 18~UTC without additional temporal aggregation. The fields preserve their native temporal meanings: u10, v10, t2m, d2m, sp and tcc represent instantaneous conditions; tp, lsp, e and ssr represent preceding-hour accumulations; and mxtpr and mntpr represent the maximum and minimum precipitation rates within the preceding hour \citep{ecmwf_era5_documentation}.
+
+These variables were aligned by their valid timestamps and spatially averaged over the study domain. No conversion to a common temporal window was performed. In the main forecasting pipeline, predictor values were standardized using training-period statistics, which does not alter their temporal meanings.
+
+The latest input at $t_0$ therefore does not directly include a value from the target hour $(t_0+5\,\mathrm{h},t_0+6\,\mathrm{h}]$. We have clarified these distinctions in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset} and acknowledge the omitted hourly evolution and retrospective reanalysis setting in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset}: added a short paragraph grouping the candidate variables by native temporal meaning and distinguishing standardization from temporal conversion. Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:variables} remains unchanged. Existing input/target definitions in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:forecasting_models} and sampling/availability limitations in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations} are retained. Final page and line numbers pending full-manuscript assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Native temporal meanings (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The sampled values retain their native temporal meanings: u10, v10, t2m, d2m, sp and tcc are instantaneous at the valid time; tp, lsp, e and ssr are accumulations over the preceding hour; and mxtpr and mntpr are within-hour extrema of precipitation rate, not hourly-mean rates \citep{ecmwf_era5_documentation}. We aligned these fields by valid timestamp without converting them to a common averaging window. Apart from the tp target conversion described above, the selected predictors retain the native units listed in Table~\ref{tab:variables} before training-only standardization; scaling changes numerical location and scale, not temporal support.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Valid-time alignment and availability boundary (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{For the retrospective input sequence, every predictor has a valid time at or before the forecast origin $t_0$. Large-scale precipitation (lsp) is accumulated over the hour ending at its valid time, while mxtpr and mntpr are the maximum and minimum total precipitation rates within that hour. These precipitation inputs and the tp target come from ERA5 short forecasts initialized at 06 and 18~UTC; the instantaneous inputs sp, tcc, v10 and d2m are supplied as analysis fields in the CDS product \citep{ecmwf_era5_documentation}. Thus, the latest precipitation input represents $(t_0-1\,\mathrm{h},t_0]$, whereas the target represents $(t_0+5\,\mathrm{h},t_0+6\,\mathrm{h}]$; no target-hour precipitation value is directly included in the input window. At 00 and 12~UTC origins, however, the latest precipitation input and target are steps 6 and 12 of the same short-forecast cycle. Feature screening and standardization use training-period data only. Valid-time ordering does not establish that the reanalysis inputs were available in real time at $t_0$.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Six-hour subsampling limitation (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The 6-hour subsampling of the hourly ERA5 data introduces a related constraint. Rapidly developing precipitation systems can evolve on much shorter time scales, particularly during convective events. A six-step input sequence provides 30~h of historical information but cannot resolve sub-hourly or hourly changes in local rainfall structure. Future work could examine whether higher-frequency ERA5 data or complementary radar and satellite observations improve the representation of short-duration events.}
+}
 
