 \ReviewerComment{f. How do you distinguish the seasonal influence of predictors? In other words, is v10 a good predictor all throughout the year? The fact that u10 did not show up in your list of predictors indicates that the meridional influence (v10 with its origin mostly in the tropics) is more important than the zonal influence. This remote influence has a seasonal cycle.}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{We distinguish seasonal statistical association from model-specific predictive contribution. Training-only q09 diagnostics pair $x(t)$ with hourly precipitation at $t+6$~h and assign DJF/MAM/JJA/SON by target valid time. v10 has positive lead-time Spearman correlations in every season (0.200--0.278), and positive rank-partial correlations controlling only current precipitation (0.150--0.205). These descriptive estimates support year-round association, not a stable year-round performance gain or formally tested seasonal contrasts (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3f_seasonal_associations}).
+
+The original eleven-candidate contemporaneous screening ranked v10 seventh and u10 eleventh. The annual lead-time correlation of u10 is -0.058, but DJF and SON estimates are -0.123 and -0.145; MAM/JJA estimates are +0.039/-0.046. Thus annual screening can conceal season-specific signals and does not prove that zonal flow is physically unimportant. The previously reported v10 top-seven bootstrap frequency of 0.797 applies to the expanded thirteen-candidate lead-time pool; it is 1.000 in the original eleven-candidate pool. We retain the original seven model inputs and the existing R2-M3 analysis.
+
+Saved q13 seasonal summaries of matched q10 ablations provide model-level evidence (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3f_seasonal_v10_ablation}). For both attention architectures, removing v10 increases DJF/SON RMSE in all five seeds, but decreases MAM RMSE in all five; JJA seed-t intervals include zero. For BiLSTM-Attention, the four seasonal mean differences are +0.005530, -0.002521, +0.000952 and +0.004268~mm\,h$^{-1}$. These pointwise seed-t intervals are not adjusted for multiple comparisons and do not capture test-sampling uncertainty; no seasonal ablation block-bootstrap intervals were saved. The short test period and two tested architectures limit generalization. We found seasonal ablation evidence, rather than a saved season-specific SHAP analysis.
+
+Finally, v10 is a local meridional wind component, not an air-mass source label. We did not perform trajectory, moisture-source or circulation-composite analyses and cannot attribute its signal primarily to the tropics. The results therefore qualify the year-round interpretation without establishing a remote physical mechanism. We added a compact Results paragraph and two supplementary tables; previously closed analyses remain unchanged.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:predictor_selection_results}: added seasonal association and matched-ablation findings with their limitations. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3f_seasonal_associations} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3f_seasonal_v10_ablation}: new training-season association and test-season v10 ablation summaries, respectively. Existing R2-M3 tables and model inputs are unchanged. Final page/line numbers pending full assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Seasonal association versus model contribution (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:predictor_selection_results})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Seasonal training-only diagnostics show positive v10 lead-time correlations in all four seasons (0.200--0.278) and positive rank-partial correlations controlling current precipitation (0.150--0.205). The annual u10 lead-time correlation (-0.058) masks negative DJF and SON estimates (-0.123 and -0.145), with smaller MAM and JJA estimates (+0.039 and -0.046); t2m changes from +0.204 in DJF to -0.130 in JJA, and sp is more negative in MAM/JJA than DJF (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3f_seasonal_associations}). These are descriptive seasonal estimates, without formal between-season tests. Model-level evidence differs from marginal association: removing v10 from BiLSTM-Attention changes seasonal RMSE by +0.005530, -0.002521, +0.000952 and +0.004268~mm\,h$^{-1}$ in DJF, MAM, JJA and SON, respectively. Both attention architectures show positive removal effects in all five seeds in DJF/SON, negative effects in all five in MAM, and seed-t intervals spanning zero in JJA (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3f_seasonal_v10_ablation}). These conditional diagnostics cover only two architectures and two to three test-period realizations per season; no seasonal ablation block-bootstrap intervals were saved. Neither the annual screening nor these seasonal associations establish the relative physical importance of zonal and meridional flow, or a tropical origin of the v10 signal.}
+}
 
