 \ReviewerComment{L329-333: Since a large part of the precipitation is linked to large-scale circulation (advection), the historical window chosen to build the model should be linked in some way to the mean length of a passing storm. Is your choice of 30 h window linked to this, or is it arbitrary? Please justify.}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{We agree that the history length requires justification. The original specification used six snapshots at $t_0-30$, $t_0-24$, $t_0-18$, $t_0-12$, $t_0-6$ and $t_0$ hours, not thirty consecutive hourly inputs. We found no verifiable record linking that choice to measured storm-passage durations or a prior systematic window search. We therefore describe it as the original modelling setting, without claiming a physically determined or optimal window.
+
+Existing sensitivity runs compare 12 h versus 30 h for six models on 3,939 common test targets, with five matched seeds and unchanged within-model hyperparameters/training protocol (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models}). All six have lower mean RMSE with 12 h; BiLSTM-Attention changes from 0.2429 to 0.2410~mm\,h$^{-1}$. It and XGBoost retain the top two positions, but the BU-LSTM variants exchange positions. The extended two-model comparison uses histories of 6, 12, 18, 30, 42 and 66 h on 3,933 common targets; BiLSTM-Attention is numerically best at 6 h and XGBoost at 12 h (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve}). Thus 30 h is not established as optimal. Longer histories do not give a consistent all-target gain, although some numerical and subset improvements occur.
+
+The tables distinguish paired seed-t intervals from block intervals based on seed-averaged squared errors; these are different estimands and all intervals are pointwise, without multiplicity adjustment. Training/validation sample counts vary with the window. Transformer optimization may also be constrained by the common epoch cap. The temporal SHAP summaries in Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_temporal_bilstm_attn} and~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_temporal_bulstm_attn} provide auxiliary model-level evidence of concentration toward recent snapshots, but cannot identify storm duration or the physical timescale of advection. We retain the original window for the main experiments and report its sensitivity explicitly.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:forecasting_models}: window rationale and sensitivity design; Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_results}: window sensitivity results and limits. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve}: existing six-model/two-model evidence, respectively. Original model settings and previously closed comments remain unchanged. Final page and line numbers pending full assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Window rationale (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:forecasting_models})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The six-snapshot (30 h) history is the original modelling specification, rather than a window justified by measured storm-passage durations. We found no independently verifiable record linking its initial choice to such durations, a specific literature recommendation or a systematic window search; its pre-experimental selection history is therefore not established. Retrospective sensitivity checks compare 12 h and 30 h for six models, and 6, 12, 18, 30, 42 and 66 h for XGBoost and BiLSTM-Attention, using five matched seeds and common test times within each comparison (Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve}). Shorter windows yield more training/validation samples, so these are sensitivity checks of the full window specification, not comparisons with identical fitted sample sets.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Window sensitivity and interpretation (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_results})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Input-history sensitivity does not establish 30 h as optimal. On 3,939 common test targets, changing from 30 h to 12 h lowers mean RMSE for all six tested models; BiLSTM-Attention changes from 0.2429 to 0.2410~mm\,h$^{-1}$ and Transformer from 0.2600 to 0.2468~mm\,h$^{-1}$. BiLSTM-Attention and XGBoost remain first and second, while BU-LSTM and BU-LSTM-Attention exchange positions; these are q14 reruns, not replacements for the original main-table runs. The wider two-model comparison uses 3,933 common targets: BiLSTM-Attention has its lowest numerical test RMSE with 6 h (0.2406 versus 0.2430 for 30 h), whereas XGBoost is lowest with 12 h. BiLSTM-Attention at 42/66 h improves numerically over 30 h, but both all-target interval types include zero; longer histories therefore do not show a consistent all-target advantage. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_six_models} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3window_curve} report seed variability and the separate block-sampling diagnostic without multiplicity adjustment. The Transformer comparison retains the 30-epoch cap and cannot isolate window length from optimization effects. Temporal SHAP concentrated toward recent snapshots is a conditional model diagnostic, not evidence for storm lifetime or a physical advection timescale.}
+}
 
