 \ReviewerComment{L341-344: Is your climatology seasonally dependent? In other words, do you use the climatology of July for the month of July and that of January for the month of January? If not, why?}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{The original climatology was not seasonally dependent: it used one overall training-period mean for every forecast, so July and January received the same constant. It was a simple reference, not a month-specific climatology; we found no separate documented design rationale for preferring it to a seasonal reference.
+
+We have added training-fitted UTC-hour, seasonal (DJF/MAM/JJA/SON), monthly and month-by-hour climatologies (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:benchmark_models}). In the monthly version, July forecasts use the training-period July mean and January forecasts the training-period January mean; month-by-hour forecasts additionally match UTC hour. The fitted means remain fixed during validation and testing.
+
+Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:strong_baselines} already reports their RMSEs on the same 3,939 test targets: 0.273760, 0.273018, 0.272957 and 0.273057~mm\,h$^{-1}$, respectively, versus 0.273693 for the refitted overall mean. This refit uses 18,404 training targets and is distinct from the original saved constant in Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:seed_stability}, fitted to 18,410 raw training values. Thus seasonal/monthly conditioning changes overall RMSE only slightly here; the comparison is no longer based solely on the original constant. The reported skill-score denominator is month-by-hour climatology, not whichever climatology minimizes test RMSE. Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:stronger_baseline_results} also retains the stronger statistical references and their qualified conclusions. These aggregate scores do not imply an absence of seasonal precipitation variation or replace season-specific evaluation.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Existing Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:benchmark_models} defines the climatologies; Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:stronger_baseline_results} and Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:strong_baselines} report the matched test comparison. Only this response and its supporting excerpts are added; the existing manuscript and supplementary tables are unchanged. Final page/line numbers pending full assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Training-fitted conditional climatologies (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:benchmark_models})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The original climatology uses the overall training-period mean. Additional climatologies use training-period conditional means grouped by forecast valid-time UTC hour, season (DJF, MAM, JJA or SON), calendar month, or month and UTC hour jointly. These fitted means are frozen for validation and testing.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Matched climatology comparison (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:stronger_baseline_results})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Full-test RMSE was 0.273693~mm\,h$^{-1}$ for the refitted overall-mean climatology, 0.273760 for UTC-hour climatology, 0.273018 for seasonal climatology, 0.272957 for monthly climatology and 0.273057 for month-by-hour climatology. Persistence had RMSE 0.328187~mm\,h$^{-1}$, compared with 0.262659 for AR(1), 0.249733 for Ridge L1, 0.253177 for Ridge L2 and 0.262894 for OLS L3. Seasonal and monthly conditioning therefore changed climatological RMSE only slightly in this dataset; month-by-hour climatology remains the specified skill-score reference rather than selecting the best climatology on the test set.}
+}
 
