 \ReviewerComment{L376: Since precipitation contains lots of zeros and few extremes as you acknowledge in L460-462, why not use Huber loss instead of MSE when optimizing the model?}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\makeatletter
+\expandafter\let\csname r@sec:training_objective_sensitivity\expandafter\endcsname\csname r@manuscript-sec:training_objective_sensitivity\endcsname
+\makeatother
+
+\AuthorResponse{MSE is aligned with our conditional-mean point forecasts and RMSE evaluation. Huber reduces the influence of large residuals, which are not necessarily all heavy-rainfall cases; it does not by itself resolve excess zeros or guarantee better upper-tail prediction. We now explain this in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:bilstm_attention_components}.
+
+We tested standard Huber with $\delta=0.5,1,2$ in standardized target units for BiLSTM-Attention and Transformer, using the same split, six-snapshot inputs, within-model hyperparameters and five fixed seeds. Both selected $\delta=2$ by mean validation RMSE among the Huber candidates; this boundary choice is not a proven optimum. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_scores} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_pairs} report all candidates and regime scores. For BiLSTM-Attention, selected Huber increases overall RMSE by 0.0040 and upper-tail RMSE by 0.0450~mm\,h$^{-1}$, with no seed improving. Its prediction SD ratio falls from 0.414 to 0.327. Conversely, Transformer overall RMSE improves by 0.0057~mm\,h$^{-1}$ with all five seeds improving; its wet/upper-tail seed intervals include zero, and both losses frequently approach the shared 30-epoch cap. These findings are conditional on the tested training budget.
+
+The separate XGBoost comparison includes pseudo-Huber, log1p, Tweedie and two weighting schemes (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_xgb}). All have five seed records, but metrics are calculated from seed-averaged predictions, not averaged seed scores. Pseudo-Huber is distinct from standard Huber; weighting reduces upper-tail error at a cost in overall error and bias. These tests do not identify the dominant cause of compressed forecasts or show that Huber is generally unsuitable for precipitation. We retain the model-specific results and limitations in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:training_objective_sensitivity}; all reported intervals are pointwise and unadjusted for multiple comparisons.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:bilstm_attention_components}: concise MSE/Huber rationale. Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:training_objective_sensitivity}: added table references and descriptive compression diagnostics; existing results and Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations} limitations retained. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_scores}, \ref{supp-stab:r3huber_pairs} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_xgb}: complete q15 and distinct q05f evidence. Final page/line numbers pending full assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{MSE rationale (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:bilstm_attention_components})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{MSE targets the conditional mean under squared-error risk and is aligned with RMSE evaluation. Huber loss is quadratic near zero residual and linear for sufficiently large residuals, reducing their leverage; those residuals need not all correspond to upper-tail rainfall. It therefore neither automatically addresses excess zeros nor guarantees improved tail forecasts. The matched validation-selected sensitivity analysis in Section~\ref{sec:training_objective_sensitivity} evaluates this choice empirically, rather than assuming one loss is universally preferable.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Matched loss-sensitivity results (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:training_objective_sensitivity})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{In the controlled five-seed BiLSTM-Attention comparison, the validation-selected Huber parameter was $\delta=2$ in standardized target units. Mean test RMSE increased from 0.2429 with MSE to 0.2468~mm\,h$^{-1}$ with Huber (paired difference $+0.0040$~mm\,h$^{-1}$), with none of the five seeds improving; upper-tail RMSE increased by 0.0450~mm\,h$^{-1}$. For the Transformer, mean RMSE decreased from 0.2600 to 0.2543~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (difference $-0.0057$~mm\,h$^{-1}$), with all five seeds improving. Both Transformer training configurations frequently approached the 30-epoch limit, so this comparison does not establish an intrinsic advantage of Huber under fully converged training. Its wet- and upper-tail RMSE differences had seed-based confidence intervals that included zero. In the XGBoost comparison, metrics were computed on seed-averaged predictions. The MSE control (M0) had overall RMSE 0.2426, upper-tail RMSE 0.8416 and overall bias $-0.0043$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Inverse-frequency weighting (M3) gave corresponding values of 0.2930, 0.6490 and $+0.1282$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Thus, overall RMSE increased by 0.0504~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (about 21\%), while upper-tail RMSE decreased by 0.1926~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The value $+0.1282$ is M3's own bias (prediction minus observation), not its bias difference from M0. No statistically supported improvement in AUC or CSI was detected; this does not mean their numerical values were unchanged. These model-specific sensitivity comparisons use their own controls and are distinct from the original main-comparison runs.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Compression diagnostics and separate experiment identities (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:training_objective_sensitivity})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_scores} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_pairs} now show MSE and all three Huber candidates, including validation selection, regime scores, paired seed effects and separate block intervals. For BiLSTM-Attention, the all-target prediction-to-observation standard-deviation ratio decreases from 0.414 with MSE to 0.327 with $\delta=2$, while upper-tail mean bias changes from -0.7642 to -0.8158~mm\,h$^{-1}$. These are descriptive distribution changes, not proof that MSE or Huber is the dominant cause of underestimation. The distinct five-seed mean-prediction XGBoost objective/weighting comparison is reported in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3huber_xgb}; it must not be pooled with the deep-model seed-level effects.}
+}
 
